Known Limitations & Trade-offs
1. Bcache Not Implemented ⚠️
Reason: NVMe partition table full (6 partitions), no space for cache partition p7.

Impact: HDD operates at native speed (~120 MB/s) instead of NVMe-cached (~2000 MB/s).

Mitigation: HDD Swap priority 5 (lowest active tier), rarely accessed. ZRAM (priority 100) handles 95%+ of swap pressure, making HDD speed irrelevant in practice.

Validation: Current usage shows 0B on HDD swap = bcache unnecessary for this workload.

2. Root Partition at 85% Capacity
Status: Was 100% full (blocked bcache-tools install), cleaned to 85% by removing old 5.9GB swapfile.

Monitoring: Should check df -h / weekly, consider repartitioning if exceeds 90%.

Risk: Low (most growth is in /home and /var which have dedicated partitions).

3. Windows Partition Preserved
Trade-off: HDD Swap limited to 792GB instead of full 931GB.

Benefit: Zero data loss, reversible changes, dual-boot capability maintained.

Assessment: 792GB swap is vastly more than needed (current usage 0B), acceptable compromise.

4. USB Dependency (Optional)
Status: USB Tier 5 is lowest priority, system functions perfectly without it.

Safety: Udev rules ensure safe removal, but recommend leaving connected for extreme scenarios.

Alternative: Can disable USB swap if port needed: sudo swapoff /dev/sdb1

